window.__registerChapter({"num": 36, "slug": "036_Bartter_and_Gitelman_Syndromes", "title": "Bartter and Gitelman Syndromes", "authors": "Victor M. Garcia Nieto, Pedro Arango Sancho, Matilde Gil Villena", "part_num": "VI", "part_title": "Renal Disorders", "word_count": 11226, "pearls": ["The neonatal variant is characterized by marked polyhydramnios from 22 to 24 weeks of gestation secondary to intrauterine polyuria, which may necessitate repeated amniocentesis to prevent premature delivery.", "They are thin, with triangularshaped facies characterized by a prominent forehead, large eyes, prominent, pointed ears, and a downturned mouth.", "Bartter syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in five genes encoding various transporters involved in the tubular reabsorption of Na , K , and Cl in thick ascending limb (TAL) (Fig. 36.2).", "Classic Bartter syndrome (type 3) is caused by pathogenic variants in CLCNKB , located on chromosome 1p36.", "Autosomal dominant hypocalcemia is a genetically heterogeneous condition.", "The most common symptoms are salt craving, muscle symptoms (fatigue, weakness, pain, cramps, paresthesia or numbness), palpitations, thirst (polydipsia), and the presence of low blood pressure.", "Na leaves the cell via the Na , K -ATPase (variants in the gene encoding its ɣ-subunit produce autosomal dominant hypomagnesemia with hypocalciuria and those encoding the α-subunit hypomagnesemia with seizures and mental retardation type 2).", "Mg ions pass from the tubular lumen to the cytosol via the apical TRPM6/TRPM7 channel (variants in TRPM6 and TRPM7 produce hypomagnesemia with autosomal recessive secondary hypocalcemia type 1 and type 2, respectively)."], "genes": ["ATP1A1", "BSND", "CLCNKB", "CLDN10", "FXYD2", "HNF1B", "KCNJ1", "KCNJ10", "KCNJ16", "MAGED2", "MODY5", "NKCC2", "PCBD1", "ROMK1", "SLC12A1", "SLC12A3", "TRPM6", "TRPM7"], "omims": [], "subtopics": ["Bartter Syndrome", "Clinical Manifestations", "Pathology", "Types of Bartter Syndrome, Gitelman Syndrome, and Related Conditions", "Laboratory Findings", "Special Renal Function Tests", "Pathophysiology", "Etiology—Genetic Basis", "Bartter Disease Type 1", "Bartter Disease Type 2", "Bartter Disease Type 3", "Bartter Disease Type 4", "Bartter Disease Type 5", "Autosomal Dominant Hypocalcemia Type 1 Presented as Pseudo-Bartter Syndrome"], "figs": [{"img": "fig_p0809_05.jpg", "label": "Fig. 36.1", "caption": "Diagnostic yield and disease reclassification. Patients are initially classified based on the clinical suspicion raised by referring physicians (left node). The clinical diagnosis was reframed during preWES consultation (middle node). All the patients underwent genetic testing with WES that allowed us to confirm or reclassify the clinical diagnosis (right node). BS, Bartter syndrome; GS, Gitelman syndrome; Negative, patients without pathogenic variants; Other, genetic diagnosis outside BS and GS."}, {"img": "vfig_p0808_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 36.1", "caption": "Diagnostic yield and disease reclassification. Patients are initially classified based on the clinical suspicion raised by referring physicians (left node). The clinical diagnosis was reframed during preWES consultation (middle node). All the patients underwent genetic testing with WES that allowed us to confirm or reclassify the clinical diagnosis (right node). BS, Bartter syndrome; GS, Gitelman syndrome; Negative, patients without pathogenic variants; Other, genetic diagnosis outside BS and GS."}, {"img": "fig_p0811_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 36.2", "caption": "Significant aspects of the pathophysiology of Bartter syndrome (see text)."}, {"img": "vfig_p0810_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 36.2", "caption": "Significant aspects of the pathophysiology of Bartter syndrome (see text)."}, {"img": "fig_p0812_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 36.3", "caption": "Tubular reabsorption of Na , K , and Cl in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle. The Na , K , ATPase brings K into the cell and extracts Na from the cell using the energy provided by ATP hydrolysis. The resulting concentration gradient allows the action of the ionic co-transporter, NKCC2, which brings Na , K , and Cl into the cell (variants in SLC12A1 encoding this transporter cause Bartter disease type 1). For NKCC2 to be more effective, the tubular lumen needs to be positive, so K leaves the cell (is recycled) through the action of the ROMK channel (variants in KCNJ1 encoding this channel are the cause of Bartter disease type 2). Cl leaves the cell thanks to the activity of the ClC-Ka and ClCKb chloride channels (variants in CLCNKB encoding the ClC-Kb channel cause Bartter disease type 3). For ClC-Ka and ClC-Kb to act, the activity of a β-subunit called barttin is required (variants in the gene encoding this subunit cause Bartter disease type 4, which results in deafness). A similar clinical picture occurs if there are simultaneous variants in CLCNKA and CLCNKB and is known as Bartter type 4b. Variants in the MAGED2 gene encoding melanoma-associated antigen D2 (MAGE-D2) cause transient antenatal Bartter syndrome (Bartter disease type 5). MAGED2 interacts with and Gs-alpha proteins that promotes"}, {"img": "fig_p0817_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 36.4", "caption": "Clinical manifestations associated with Gitelman disease expressed by frequency of occurrence. (Modified from Tsukamoto T, Kobayashi T, Kawamoto K, Fukase M, Chihara K. Possible discrimination of Gitelman’s syndrome from Bartter’s syndrome by renal clearance study: report of two cases. Am J Kidney Dis. 1995;25(4):637-641.)"}, {"img": "vfig_p0816_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 36.4", "caption": "Clinical manifestations associated with Gitelman disease expressed by frequency of occurrence. (Modified from Tsukamoto T, Kobayashi T, Kawamoto K, Fukase M, Chihara K. Possible discrimination of Gitelman’s syndrome from Bartter’s syndrome by renal clearance study: report of two cases. Am J Kidney Dis. 1995;25(4):637-641.)"}, {"img": "fig_p0817_10.jpg", "label": "Fig. 36.5", "caption": "Image of chondrocalcinosis of the meniscus of the left knee. (From García Nieto V, Cantabrana A, Müller D, Claverie-Martín F. [Condrocalcinosis e hipomagnesemia en un paciente portador de una nueva mutación en el gen del cotransportador de ClNa sensible a tiazidas]. Nefrología . 2003;23(6):504-509.)"}, {"img": "fig_p0819_02.jpg", "label": "Fig. 36.6", "caption": "Physiological mechanisms of transport in the distal convoluted tubule. Na and Cl pass from the tubular lumen into the cell via the thiazide-sensitive NaCl co-transporter NCC. Cl leaves the cell via the chloride channel ClC-Kb (variants in the gene cause Bartter disease type 3). Na leaves the cell via the Na , K -ATPase (variants in the gene encoding its ɣ-subunit produce autosomal dominant hypomagnesemia with hypocalciuria and those encoding the α-subunit hypomagnesemia with seizures and mental retardation type 2). Hepatocyte nuclear transcription factor 1 (HNF1β) regulates transcription of the gene encoding the ɣ-subunit of the Na , K -ATPase (variants in the coding gene produce HNF1β nephropathy). The coactivator PCBD1 (pterin-4-alpha-carbinolamine dehydratase 1) is a cofactor of HNF1β and increases the transcriptional activity of the γ-subunit of the Na , K -ATPase (variants in the coding gene produce transient neonatal Hyperphenylalaninemia with primapterinuria). FAM111A is a regulator of certain nuclear transcription factors (heterozygous missense variants in the coding gene cause Kenny-Caffey syndrome type 2, which can present with hypomagnesemia). In the distal tubule, Mg reabsorption is an active transport process by transcellular routes, driven by the potential difference between the tubular lumen and the interior of th"}, {"img": "fig_p0821_09.jpg", "label": "Fig. 36.7", "caption": "Diagnostic algorithm for hypomagnesemias based on calcemia and calciuria levels. Abnormal transporters are shown in parentheses (in most cases). (Modified from García-Nieto VM, Claverie Martín F, Moraleda-Mesa T, et al. Renal diseases that course with hypomagnesemia. Comments on a new hereditary hypomagnesemic tubulopathy. Nefrología . 2024;44(1):23-31 [Fig. 2, p. 28].)"}, {"img": "vfig_p0820_01.jpg", "label": "Fig. 36.7", "caption": "Diagnostic algorithm for hypomagnesemias based on calcemia and calciuria levels. Abnormal transporters are shown in parentheses (in most cases). (Modified from García-Nieto VM, Claverie Martín F, Moraleda-Mesa T, et al. Renal diseases that course with hypomagnesemia. Comments on a new hereditary hypomagnesemic tubulopathy. Nefrología . 2024;44(1):23-31 [Fig. 2, p. 28].)"}], "tables_meta": [{"label": "TABLE 36.1", "title": "#### Types of Bartter Syndrome, Gitelman Syndrome, and Related Conditions"}, {"label": "TABLE 36.2", "title": "Featur Variant es That Dist Age of Onset inguish Bar Serum K tter and Git Serum Cl elman Syndr Serum Mg ome Variants Serum Renin, Aldosteron"}, {"label": "TABLE 36.2", "title": "Features Variant That Dist Age of Onset inguish Bar Serum K tter and Git Serum Cl elman Syndr Serum Mg ome Variants–Serum Renin, Aldosterone"}, {"label": "Table 36.4", "title": "Leading Symptom Differential Diagnosis Additional Findings"}], "body_md": "\n<!-- p.806 -->\n\n# 36 Bartter and Gitelman Syndromes \n\n**VICTOR M. GARCÍA NIETO, PEDRO ARANGO SANCHO, MATILDE GIL VILLENA** \n\n## **Bartter Syndrome** \n\nBartter syndrome is not one entity, but a group of diseases defined by hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis, hypercalciuria, hyperaldosteronemia, and salt wasting (Table 36.1). Gitelman syndrome is phenotypically related but has a distinct pathogenic variant and biochemical laboratory findings (hypokalemic, hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis with hypomagnesemia) (Tables 36.1 and 36.2). Indeed, what may clinically appear to be one syndrome on genetic testing may actually be the other disorder (Fig. 36.1). \n\n### **Clinical Manifestations** \n\nTwo clinical patterns have been established that distinguish between a severe form of antenatal presentation **(antenatal Bartter)** and a later onset form, during early life **(classic Bartter)** . \n\nThe **neonatal variant** is characterized by marked polyhydramnios from 22 to 24 weeks of gestation secondary to intrauterine polyuria, which may necessitate repeated amniocentesis to prevent premature delivery. At birth, severe dehydration episodes, hypercalciuria, and earlyonset nephrocalcinosis are noted. Some patients have a unique physical appearance. They are thin, with triangularshaped facies characterized by a prominent forehead, large eyes, prominent, pointed ears, and a downturned mouth. Sensorineural deafness may be present. Growth restriction is common, although it may improve with treatment. Intellectual impairment is commonly described with the neonatal onset disorder. Other systemic manifestations include seizures, increased susceptibility to infections, secretory diarrhea, and osteopenia. Characteristic features of the neonatal variants are hypercalciuria, nephrocalcinosis, and hyperprostaglandinuria E2. Prenatal diagnosis can be suspected by the detection of elevated Cl<sup>−</sup> concentration in the amniotic fluid. \n\nIn the **classic variant** , a history of maternal polyhydramnios and preterm birth may also be present. It usually \n\npresents during the first two years of life and the symptoms include polyuria, polydipsia, vomiting, chronic tendency to dehydration, constipation, excessive salt cravings, growth failure, and developmental delays. Over time, other symptoms such as asthenia, muscle weakness, muscle cramps, and episodes of carpopedal spasms may appear. Cognitive involvement is variable, from mild learning difficulties to more severe intellectual disability. Nephrocalcinosis is rare and hypercalciuria is less severe than in the neonatal form. Hypokalemia may manifest as muscle weakness, fatigue, and flaccid quadriparesis. If prolonged and significant, the formation of renal cysts can occur. \n\n_In both variants, blood pressure is always normal or tends to be reduced._ \n\n### **Pathology** \n\nRenal histology is characteristic. The glomeruli show marked hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the juxtaglomerular apparatus; juxtaglomerular hypertrophy may be absent despite the presence of all other features of the syndrome. All three parts of the juxtaglomerular apparatus are usually affected: the epithelial cells of the afferent arteriole wall, the macula densa of the distal tubule and the lacis (a group of extraglomerular mesangial cells located in the triangular region formed by the afferent arteriole, efferent arteriole and distal tubule). Increased renin synthesis is observed in the epithelial cells of the afferent arteriole. Additional findings include the exaggerated presence of immature glomeruli which are small in size, relatively acellular, and covered with cuboidal cells with scant cytoplasm. The muscular walls of small arteries may appear hypertrophic, not only in the kidney but also in other organs. These vascular lesions appear to worsen as the disease progresses despite good therapeutic control. An important finding is the presence of hyperplasia of renomedullary interstitial cells, the likely site of prostaglandin production. In advanced cases, variable degrees of glomerular hyalinosis, tubular atrophy, and interstitial fibrosis may be observed. _Renal biopsy is not indicated in patients with Bartter syndrome_ .\n\n<!-- p.807 -->\n\n##### **TABLE 36.1** \n\n#### **Types of Bartter Syndrome, Gitelman Syndrome, and Related Conditions** \n\n|Disorder|Gene|Gene Product|Inheritance|Features|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|**BS Variants**|||||\n|BS I (ABS, HPES)|_SLC12A1_|NKCC2|AR|Polyhydramnios, prematurity, hypokalemic<br>hypochloremic alkalosis, nephrocalcinosis,<br>with or without concentrating defect|\n|BS II (ABS with transient<br>hyperkalemia and<br>acidosis, HPES)|_KCNJ1_|ROMK1|AR|Polyhydramnios, prematurity, transient<br>hyperkalemia and acidosis, then hypokalemic<br>hypochloremic alkalosis, nephrocalcinosis,<br>with or without concentrating defect|\n|BS III (CBS)|_CLCNKB_|ClC-Kb|AR; many<br>sporadic|Variable age at presentation with severity<br>corresponding to type of gene pathogenic<br>variant; hypokalemic hypochloremic alkalosis|\n|BS IVa and BS IVb<br>(ABS or HPES with<br>sensorineural deafness)|_BSND_<br>_CLCNKA,_<br>_CLCNKB_|Bartter<br>ClC-Ka and<br>ClC-Kb|AR|Polyhydramnios, prematurity, hypokalemic<br>hypochloremic alkalosis, sensorineural<br>deafness, with or without concentrating defect|\n|BS V (transient ABS)|_MAGED2_|MAGED2|XR|Severe polyhydramnios, hypokalemic<br>hypochloremic alkalosis with symptoms<br>resolving within the first few months of life|\n|AD hypocalcemic<br>hypercalciuria|_L125P_|CaSR|AD|Hypocalcemic hypocalciuria, hypokalemic<br>hypochloremic alkalosis, suppressed PTH|\n|**GS Variants**|||||\n|GS|_SLC12A3_|NCC|AR|Present in later childhood or adulthood with<br>weakness, lethargy, carpopedal spasm,<br>hypokalemic alkalosis, hypomagnesemia,<br>hypermagnesuria, and hypocalciuria|\n|EAST syndrome<br>(SeSAME)|_Kir4.1_|KCNJ10|AR|Epilepsy, ataxia, sensorineural deafness,<br>hypokalemic hypochloremic alkalosis|\n|**Other Variants**|||||\n|_CLDN10_pathogenic<br>variants|_CLDN10_|Claudin-10|AR|Hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis with<br>hypocalciuria but normal to elevated magnesium|\n\n\n_ABS,_ Antenatal Bartter syndrome; _AD,_ autosomal dominant; _AR,_ autosomal recessive; _BS,_ Bartter syndrome; _CaSR,_ calcium-sensing receptor; _CBS,_ classic Bartter syndrome; _ClC-Ka,_ chloride channel-Ka; _ClC-Kb,_ chloride channel-Kb; _GS,_ Gitelman syndrome; _HPES,_ hyperprostaglandin E syndrome; _MAGED,_ melanoma-associated antigen-D2; _NCC,_ thiazide-sensitive NaCl co-transporter; _NKCC2,_ furosemide-sensitive Na-K-2Cl co-transporter; _OMIM,_ Online Mendelian Inheritance in Man; _PTH,_ parathyroid hormone; _ROMK,_ renal outer medullary K channel; _SeSAME,_ seizures, sensorineural deafness, ataxia, mental retardation, and electrolyte imbalances; _XR,_ X-linked recessive. \n\nFrom Fulchiero R, Seo-Mayer P. Bartter syndrome and Gitelman syndrome. _Pediatr Clin North Am_ . 2019;66:121–134. Box 1. \n\n\n#### **TABLE 36.2** Featur Variant es That Dist Age of Onset inguish Bar Serum K tter and Git Serum Cl elman Syndr Serum Mg ome Variants Serum Renin, Aldosterone Urine Ca/Cr Other Distinct Features\n\n|BS I|AN|Low|Low|Normal|High, high|High|—|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|BS II|AN|High, then<br>low|Low|Normal|High, high|High|Transient<br>hyperkalemia|\n|BS III|N, C, A|Low|Very low|Normal|High, high|Low, normal,<br>or high|—|\n|BS IVa, IVb|AN|Low|Low|Normal|High, high|Normal or<br>high|Sensorineural<br>deafness|\n\n\n_(Continued)_\n\n<!-- p.808 -->\n\n\n#### **TABLE 36.2** Features Variant That Dist Age of Onset inguish Bar Serum K tter and Git Serum Cl elman Syndr Serum Mg ome Variants–Serum Renin, Aldosterone cont'd Urine Ca/Cr Other Distinct Features\n\n|BS V|AN|Low|Low|Normal|High, high|—|Transient features|\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n|Hypocalcemic<br>hypercalciuria|—|Low|Low|Normal|High, high|High|Family history,<br>hypocalcemia,<br>suppressed PTH|\n|GS|C, A|Low|Low|Low|High, high|Low|—|\n|EAST syndrome|—|Low|Low|Low|High, high|Low|Epilepsy, ataxia,<br>sensorineural<br>deafness|\n\n\n_A,_ Adult; _AN,_ antenatal; _BS,_ Bartter syndrome; _C,_ child; _Ca/Cr,_ spot calcium to creatinine ratio; _GS,_ Gitelman syndrome; _Mg,_ magnesium; _N,_ neonate; _PTH,_ parathyroid hormone. \n\nFrom Fulchiero R, Seo-Mayer P. Bartter syndrome and Gitelman syndrome. _Pediatr Clin North Am_ . 2019;66:121–134. Box 3. \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0809_05.jpg)\n\n\n\n![](figures/vfig_p0808_01.jpg)\n\n**Fig. 36.1** Diagnostic yield and disease reclassification. Patients are initially classified based on the clinical suspicion raised by referring physicians (left node). The clinical diagnosis was reframed during preWES consultation (middle node). All the patients underwent genetic testing with WES that allowed us to confirm or reclassify the clinical diagnosis (right node). _BS,_ Bartter syndrome; _GS,_ Gitelman syndrome; _Negative,_ patients without pathogenic variants; _Other,_ genetic diagnosis outside BS and GS.  (From Palazzo V, Raglianti V, Landini S, et al. Clinical and genetic characterization of patients with Bartter and Gitelman syndrome. _Int J Mol Sci_ . 2022;23:5641 [Fig. 1].) \n\n### **Laboratory Findings** \n\nThe fundamental biochemical finding is the presence of hypokalemia, which can be critically low, below 2 mEq/L of K<sup>+</sup> . It is frequently associated with metabolic alkalosis and hypochloremia. Rarely, it may be accompanied by metabolic acidosis, mimicking renal tubular acidosis. Hyperuricemia is common due to the contraction of extracellular volume \n\nand can be observed in 50% of patients. Moderate hypomagnesemia may also be detected and may sometimes be significant enough to require magnesium supplementation. Other reported laboratory findings include hypercalcemia, hypophosphatemia, hyperlipidemia/hypercholesterolemia, polycythemia, and impaired platelet aggregation. \n\nA characteristic feature is the demonstration of stimulation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone axis, with elevated\n\n<!-- p.809 -->\n\nplasma renin activity and aldosterone secretion. On rare occasions, aldosterone levels are increased moderately despite marked elevation of plasma renin, presumably the result of suppression of aldosterone production by hypokalemia. When K<sup>+</sup> depletion is corrected, aldosterone rises to pathological levels. The most relevant urinary finding is excessive K<sup>+</sup> loss in relation to hypokalemia. The presence of marked hyperchloruria is important in establishing the differential diagnosis with other hypokalemic states secondary to repeated vomiting or other causes of Cl<sup>−</sup> deficiency in which urinary chloride is very low. \n\nSalt (NaCl) wasting is very common. The amount directly correlates to the compensatory capacity of the most distal nephrons. Bartter syndrome typically manifests during the first years of life. In more attenuated phenotypes, older children and adolescents are able to preserve sodium when subjected to sodium restriction. Calciuria may be increased, normal, or reduced. \n\nUncommon findings are the presence of tubular proteinuria and hyperaminoaciduria. Glomerular filtration remains normal for many years. Some patients progress to end-stage chronic kidney disease. In this circumstance, focal and segmental glomerulosclerosis are frequently found. Thickening and sclerosis of small arteries and arterioles could presumably lead to decreased glomerular perfusion. Other factors such as chronic hypokalemia and elevated circulating angiotensin II may be responsible for the development of tubulointerstitial nephritis and eventual deterioration of glomerular function. \n\n### **Special Renal Function Tests** \n\nA consistent alteration is a defect in the urinary concentrating ability. Urine volume does not decrease after administration of vasopressin but does after administration of dibutyrylAMP-cyclic. This finding indicates an endogenous tubular defect in the formation of this mediator, probably dependent on the inhibitory effect of prostaglandins on tubular adenylyl cyclase. In addition, the direct effect of hypokalemia on renal concentrating capacity must be considered. \n\nPatients with neonatal Bartter syndrome have an absent natriuretic response after the administration of furosemide. \n\nAnother important finding in the disorder is the existence of an elevated urinary pH with increased ammoniuria during acid overload. This phenomenon is due to the stimulatory effect of hypokalemia on ammoniagenesis and is reversible by the administration of indomethacin. \n\nResistance to the pressor effect of angiotensin infusion was thought to be a discerning feature of the syndrome. These patients require much higher doses of angiotensin than those required in normal subjects to raise diastolic blood pressure by at least 20 mm Hg. The pressor response, however, improves after volume expansion of the extracellular space. \n\nHypotonic saline infusions assess the handling of Na<sup>+</sup> , K<sup>+</sup> , and Cl<sup>−</sup> ions in the proximal and distal segments of the \n\nrenal tubules, in the _theoretical_ absence of the hormonal influence of aldosterone and vasopressin. The most important clinical application lies in the study of tubulopathies in which there is salt loss and/or hypokalemia. This test can inform whether the salt loss occurs in the proximal or distal portions of the nephron. The inhibition of aldosterone and vasopressin is achieved by an overload with a hypo-osmolar fluid that causes an acute expansion of the extracellular volume. It is the test that studies renal dilution capacity par excellence. Theoretically, the composition of the urine corresponding to the period of maximum free water, when the test result is given, is not modified as it passes through the collecting tubule. Under these conditions, free water formation should be maximal and tubular reabsorption of electrolytes can be quantitatively estimated by calculating fractional clearances (C) in mL/100 mL of glomerular filtrate. Without the stimulation of antidiuretic hormone and aldosterone, the sodium chloride excreted in urine is equal to that leaving the proximal tubule minus that which is reabsorbed (without simultaneous water reabsorption) in the ascending limb of the loop of Henle (dilutional segment). The fluid leaving the proximal tubule is isosmotic with plasma. Each time that Na<sup>+</sup> is reabsorbed in the dilutional segment, solute-free water (CH2O) is generated. The calculation of CH2O+CNa or CH2O+CCl estimates the distal Na<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>−</sup> supply. The terms CH2O/CH2O+CNa and CH2O/CH2O+CCl reflect the distal reabsorption of Na<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>−</sup> , respectively. \n\nWith this test, the salt loss in Bartter syndrome is of distal renal tubular origin. The distal Na<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>−</sup> reabsorption defect is permanent, but in some pediatric patients a transient proximal loss of both ions has been observed. \n\n### **Pathophysiology** \n\nBartter syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in five genes encoding various transporters involved in the tubular reabsorption of Na<sup>+</sup> , K<sup>+</sup> , and Cl<sup>−</sup> in thick ascending limb (TAL) (Fig. 36.2). Salt loss causes contraction of the extracellular space, which stimulates the renin-angiotensinaldosterone axis. The latter stimulates the function of the epithelial Na<sup>+</sup> channel (ENaC) and promotes the exchange of Na<sup>+</sup> with K<sup>+</sup> and H<sup>+</sup> in the collecting duct, resulting in metabolic alkalosis and further reducing hypokalemia. This, in turn, stimulates production of the prostaglandins PGE2 and prostacyclin (PGI2) which increase potassium secretion mainly by stimulating renin secretion and activating the renin-angiotensin system, leading to increased aldosterone secretion. PGE2 decreases sodium reabsorption in TAL by inhibiting the KCC2 co-transporter and the ROMK channel (Fig. 36.3). The state of vascular resistance seems to be directly related to the hypersecretion of prostaglandins of renal origin. Under normal circumstances, as the tubular lumen is positive because of gradient effect, intercellular reabsorption of Ca<sup>++</sup> and Mg<sup>++</sup> is favored, thanks to the joint action of claudins 16 and 19 (Fig. 36.3). In Bartter syndrome, because the lumen is no longer positive, tubular\n\n<!-- p.810 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0811_02.jpg)\n\n\n\n![](figures/vfig_p0810_01.jpg)\n\n**Fig. 36.2** Significant aspects of the pathophysiology of Bartter syndrome (see text). \n\nreabsorption of both ions is reduced. The result in the urinary excretion of Ca<sup>++</sup> and Mg<sup>++</sup> will depend on the load offered to the distal portions of the nephron and their compensatory capacity. \n\n### **Etiology—Genetic Basis** \n\nMolecular biology studies have revealed Bartter syndrome to be a heterogeneous disorder caused by a combined defect in the tubular reabsorption of sodium, potassium, and chloride. Under physiological conditions, the water-free reabsorption of these ions in TAL is very complex. An error in the function of any of the proteins involved in this process causes this tubulopathy. \n\n### **Bartter Disease Type 1** \n\nPatients with neonatal Bartter syndrome have variants in _SLC12A1_ located on chromosome 15q15-21 encoding the bumetanideand furosemide-sensitive Na-K-2Cl co-transporter (BSC1 or NKCC2) (Bartter type 1) (Fig. 36.3). The normal function of the Na-K-2Cl co-transporter is to reabsorb about 30% of the filtered \n\nClNa; it is not surprising that a disturbance in its function results in substantial salt loss and volume contraction. \n\nNKCC2 is a 121 kDa membrane protein comprising 12 transmembrane domains that form a transport pathway by dimerization of two homologous domains. NKCC2 promotes the electroneutral transport of one Na<sup>+</sup> ion, one K<sup>+</sup> ion and two Cl<sup>−</sup> ions across the apical membrane of the TAL. It is the main salt reabsorption pathway in the kidney. Loop diuretics such as furosemide and bumetanide bind to portions of transmembrane domains 11 and 12, while portions of transmembrane domains 2, 4 and 7 are involved in ion transport. Numerous variants have been reported in _SLC12A1_ , mostly missense or frameshift variants that are distributed throughout all the encoded protein. NKCC2 phosphorylation and activity is regulated by uromodulin, the most abundant protein in normal urine. Late disease onset has been reported in two adolescent patients who were carriers of compound heterozygous variants. \n\n### **Bartter Disease Type 2** \n\nOther patients with a similar clinical course of neonatal Bartter disease have variants in _KCNJ1_ , located on chromosome\n\n<!-- p.811 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0812_01.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 36.3** Tubular reabsorption of Na<sup>+</sup> , K<sup>+</sup> , and Cl<sup>−</sup> in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle. The Na<sup>+</sup> , K<sup>+</sup> , ATPase brings K<sup>+</sup> into the cell and extracts Na<sup>+</sup> from the cell using the energy provided by ATP hydrolysis. The resulting concentration gradient allows the action of the ionic co-transporter, NKCC2, which brings Na<sup>+</sup> , K<sup>+</sup> , and Cl<sup>−</sup> into the cell (variants in _SLC12A1_ encoding this transporter cause Bartter disease type 1). For NKCC2 to be more effective, the tubular lumen needs to be positive, so K<sup>+</sup> leaves the cell (is recycled) through the action of the ROMK channel (variants in _KCNJ1_ encoding this channel are the cause of Bartter disease type 2). Cl<sup>−</sup> leaves the cell thanks to the activity of the ClC-Ka and ClCKb chloride channels (variants in _CLCNKB_ encoding the ClC-Kb channel cause Bartter disease type 3). For ClC-Ka and ClC-Kb to act, the activity of a β-subunit called barttin is required (variants in the gene encoding this subunit cause Bartter disease type 4, which results in deafness). A similar clinical picture occurs if there are simultaneous variants in _CLCNKA_ and _CLCNKB_ and is known as Bartter type 4b. Variants in the _MAGED2_ gene encoding melanoma-associated antigen D2 (MAGE-D2) cause transient antenatal Bartter syndrome (Bartter disease type 5). MAGED2 interacts with and Gs-alpha proteins that promotes NKCC2 and NCC insertion into the plasma membrane. In the presence of variant MAGE-D2, these interactions do not occur, resulting in degradation and lower levels of these two co-transporters. Under normal circumstances, as the tubular lumen is positive by gradient effect, intercellular reabsorption of Ca<sup>++</sup> and Mg<sup>+</sup> + is allowed, thanks to the joint action of claudins 16 and 19. Variants in the genes encoding these proteins cause familial hypomagnesaemia with hypercalciuria and nephrocalcinosis. Variants in _CLDN10_ encoding claudin-10 cause HELIX syndrome (Hypohidrosis-Electrolyte imbalanceLacrimal gland dysfunction-Ichthyosis-Xerostomia syndrome). \n\n11q24-25 encoding the ATP-sensitive potassium channel that recycles potassium into the tubular lumen (ROMK or Kir1.1) (Bartter type 2) (Fig. 36.3). The ROMK channel is present in the distal nephron and is responsible for potassium recycling in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle and potassium secretion in the cortical collecting duct. When the ability to recycle potassium from the cells to the lumen is lost, the luminal potassium concentration is too low to permit activity of the Na-K-2Cl co-transporter. \n\nInactivating variants in _KCNJ1_ include missense and nonsense changes as well as frameshifts and deletions. Functional studies of variant ROMK proteins show that the loss of function is caused by altered channel function or impaired plasma membrane abundance. ROMK is an inwardly rectifying renal K<sup>+</sup> channel that is critical for K<sup>+</sup> recycling in the TAL and K<sup>+</sup> secretion in the connecting tubule and collecting duct. ROMK channels are assembled from four subunits, each consisting of two transmembrane domains flanking a conserved loop that contributes to the\n\n<!-- p.812 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0813_01.jpg)\n\n\npore, selectivity filter, cytoplasmic NH2, and COOH ends containing both regulatory and oligomerization domains. In TAL, ROMK plays a dual role, as it supports the transport activity of NKKC2 but also contributes to a positive transepithelial membrane potential that is important for paracellular Ca<sup>++</sup> and Mg<sup>++</sup> reabsorption (Fig. 36.3) which, as indicated above, favors the occurrence of hypercalciuria and predisposition to the development of nephrocalcinosis. \n\n### **Bartter Disease Type 3** \n\nClassic Bartter syndrome (type 3) is caused by pathogenic variants in _CLCNKB_ , located on chromosome 1p36. It encodes a renal chloride channel which is in the basolateral membrane of TAL cells (ClC-Kb) and is mainly responsible for the outflow of chloride from the cell into the blood (Fig. 36.3). A functional defect of the chloride channel is secondarily accompanied by a reduction of tubular ClNa reabsorption. A relatively large number of disease-causing variants have been published for _CLCNKB_ including frequent gene deletions but also missense, nonsense, frameshift, small insertions/deletions, and splice-site variants. ClC-Kb belongs to the CLC family of Cl<sup>−</sup> exchange channels, which includes nine isoforms in mammals. Both ClC-Kb and the closely related ClC-Ka isoform (encoded by _CLCNKA_ ) are predominantly expressed in the kidney, located in the basolateral membrane of the thin ascending limb (ClC-Ka alone), in TAL cells and in the distal convoluted tubule, as well as in the intercalated cells of the collecting duct. There is another chloride channel, ClC-Ka, on the basolateral side, being the reason why the loss of ClNa is lower than in the neonatal variant and there is less urinary calcium excretion and a lower probability of nephrocalcinosis. A weak correlation between genotype and phenotype has been described. In this variant of the disease there may be hypomagnesaemia because ClC-Kb is expressed in the distal tubule, which is the most important tubular portion for the renal management of Mg<sup>++</sup> . \n\n### **Bartter Disease Type 4** \n\nBartter syndrome with sensorineural deafness is linked to chromosome 1p31 and caused by different pathogenic variants in _BSND_ . \n\n_BSND_ encodes a protein called barttin, which contains two transmembrane domains α-helices and has both aminoand carboxy-terminal portions located intracellularly. Barttin colocalizes with ClC-Ka and ClC-Kb channels in the basolateral membranes of renal tubules in the thin and thick ascending branches of loop of Henle (Fig. 36.3) and in cells of the stria vascularis of the inner ear. Barttin is the first α-subunit to be described in a Cl<sup>−</sup> channel and acts as an essential subunit in the function of ClC-Ka and ClC-Kb channels. Co-immunoprecipitation experiments have revealed a direct protein-protein interaction of barttin with the ClC-Kb channel. Barttin functions as an activator \n\nof ClC-K chloride channels and is required for appropriate tubular salt reabsorption. The result of homozygous pathogenic variants of _BSND_ is that Cl<sup>−</sup> cannot leave the cell and therefore cannot be reabsorbed. \n\nPhenotypic expressivity is variable. Patients described in the literature have a homogeneous clinical presentation including preterm delivery, polyhydramnios, significant renal saline loss, hypokalemia, growth restriction, and delayed motor development similar to patients with antenatal Bartter syndrome. All these reported patients had chronic renal failure at the end of the first year of life. In contrast, the thirteen patients belonging to the first described families with Bartter syndrome type 4 from southern Israel did not have such an aggressive clinical course. The calculated creatinine clearance in the oldest subgroup was 95 ± 20 mL/min/1.73 m<sup>2</sup> . This suggested a different predisposition to develop kidney injury that was variant dependent within _BSND_ . \n\nTwo additional families from the northwest of the island of Tenerife with a total of five patients affected by Bartter syndrome with deafness have been described. There was no evidence of kinship between the two families, although historically this is an area with high rates of consanguinity. The same C to T change at nucleotide 139 in the coding region of exon 1 was shared in both families. The variant produces a change from glycine to arginine at position 47 (G47R), located in the second hydrophobic region of the protein, which is predicted to cross the membrane. This pathogenic variant causes an abolition of ClC-Kb76 channel function. Evaluation of this variant in a barttin and CLC-K co-expression system demonstrates a drastic reduction in the activity of the channel. These studies demonstrate that the G47R variant results in impaired transepithelial transport causing disease in homozygous individuals. \n\nWhy do patients with Bartter syndrome type 3 associated with pathogenic variants in _CLCNKB_ not have deafness? The hypothesis is that the chloride channel ClC-Ka is also expressed in the inner ear, which compensates for the lack of activity of the ClC-Kb channel. In this regard, another report identified a patient with renal salt loss and deafness, a homozygous deletion in _CLCNKB_ and a homozygous missense pathogenic variants in _CLCNKA_ without identifying significant variants in _BSND_ (Bartter type 4b). In this case, barttin indemnity could not prevent the onset of deafness, which further denotes the genetic heterogeneity of Bartter syndrome patients with deafness. \n\n### **Bartter Disease Type 5** \n\nNine different variants within _MAGED2_ located on the X chromosome (seven of which were truncating) have been identified in male patients with antenatal Bartter syndrome and marked polyhydramnios. As a result, it has been possible to define a new entity called **transient antenatal Bartter syndrome** . _MAGED2_ encodes the melanoma-associated antigen D2 (MAGE-D2). In the rat kidney, MAGE-D2 it is expressed in the thin limb of loop\n\n<!-- p.813 -->\n\nof Henle, the TAL (Fig. 36.3), the distal convoluted tubule, and collecting duct. \n\nMAGE-D2 is a ubiquitous nucleolar protein that can be rapidly released to control cellular stress, allowing for rapid cell-cycle arrest. MAGE-D2 has also been shown to interact with HSP40 and Gs-alpha proteins. HSP40 participates in quality process control as chaperone to protect proteins from degradation. Gs-alpha activates adenylate cyclases inducing AMPc generation, which promotes NKCC2 and NCC (thiazide-sensitive Na<sup>+</sup> -Cl<sup>−</sup> co-transporter that localizes to the distal convoluted tubule) insertion into the plasma membrane. In the presence of altered MAGE-D2, these interactions do not occur resulting in degradation and lower levels of these two co-transporters. It is not clear why this form of Bartter syndrome is transient, but two hypotheses have been suggested. First, the interaction between HSP40 and MAGE-D2 could function to protect the co-transporters from degradation induced by tissue hypoxia that can occur during early pregnancy, a function that is not critical later in development. Second, Gs-alpha activity might be compensated for, resulting in greater sensitivity to vasopressin and reducing the dependence of Gs α on MAGE-D2 activity, as patients age. Interestingly, other work shows that MAGE-D2 expression is inducible after acute kidney injury or stress, suggesting a role of this protein in postnatal kidney physiology. \n\n### **Autosomal Dominant Hypocalcemia Type 1 Presented as Pseudo-Bartter Syndrome** \n\nAutosomal dominant hypocalcemia is a genetically heterogeneous condition. Two types have been described. Type 1 accounts for approximately 70% of cases. It is caused by gain of function variants in _CASR_ encoding the calciumsensitive receptor which constitutively activate the receptor. In this entity the CASR in the parathyroid and renal tubule becomes more sensitive and will mistake hypocalcemia as normal and will, in turn, not stimulate PTH secretion. Hypocalcemia is mild to moderate and may be symptomatic in about half of the cases. Typical symptoms include paresthesias, carpopedal spasm, and seizures. PTH is detectable and there is hypercalciuria with its eventual complications. In TAL, activation of the calcium-sensitive receptor inhibits ROMK channel activity and, secondarily, reduces ClNa reabsorption resulting in renal salt loss, activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and hypokalemic alkalosis **(pseudo-Bartter syndrome)** . \n\n### **The Renal Salt Sensor and Bartter Syndrome** \n\nUnder physiological conditions, the macula densa located in the distal convoluted tubule is the renal salt sensor. The close contact between the macula densa and its afferent arteriole form the juxtaglomerular apparatus that will allow glomerular feedback control (tubulo-glomerular feedback, TGF). Thus, in situations where there is excessive salt supply to the macula densa, vasoconstriction occurs in the \n\nafferent arteriole via adenosine and renal glomerular filtration is reduced (vasoconstrictor TGF). Conversely, when volume depletion occurs, glomerular tubular vasodilator feedback control (vasodilator TGF) is triggered. Because there is cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) in the cells of the macula densa that increases prostaglandin synthesis, in volume depletion there is increased synthesis of PGE2 that dilates the afferent arteriole. At the same time renin synthesis is stimulated, which via angiotensin II contracts the efferent arteriole, and aldosterone synthesis is also stimulated, increasing water and sodium reabsorption in the collecting duct. The consequence is that the GRF is maintained. \n\nThe renal sensor for salt requires the integrity of the same transporter system that functions in TAL (NKCC2, ROMK, ClC-Kb) (Fig. 36.3). Therefore, in Bartter syndrome, the sensor is not functional and the macula densa misinterprets reduced tubular salt concentration as volume depletion and COX-2 is stimulated. Consequently, a vasodilator TGF (dilation of the afferent arteriole and vasoconstriction of the efferent arteriole) is chronically and erroneously produced, which increases the GRF as if there were ClNa depletion, thus favoring and worsening salt loss. Hence the efficacy of indomethacin in the treatment of patients with Bartter syndrome. \n\n### **Diagnosis** \n\nThe clinical, biochemical, and ultrasound data discussed in the previous sections provide the basis for an easy diagnostic process for Bartter syndrome. The clinical diagnosis should be confirmed by genetic analysis when possible (Fig. 36.1). The _European Rare Kidney Disease Reference Network Working Group for Tubular Disorders_ does not recommend prenatal assessment of electrolytes and/or aldosterone from amniotic fluid, since, where genetic diagnosis is not available; it is possible to use with some reliability the so-called _Bartter index_ (alpha fetoprotein × total protein). \n\nIn the postnatal period, the availability of genetic analysis makes it possible to avoid many common biochemical determinations or complex tests. The European Working Group does not recommend tubular function tests with furosemide or thiazides if genetic testing is accessible. However, this working group does not mention a basic functional test and two stimulation tests that provide good diagnostic support for the disease. The corrected volume per 100 mL of GFR is a simple calculation (plasma creatinine × 100/urinary creatinine); values higher than 1.2 mL/100 mL GFR are suggestive of polyuria. In the concentration test, a simple-to-perform test, the maximum urinary osmolality is obtained with desmopressin stimulation. All patients with Bartter syndrome have poor urine concentration at diagnosis. Hypotonic saline overload is more complex to perform but should be available in most pediatric nephrology units as it is a test that provides very clear results. A reduction in distal Na<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>−</sup> reabsorption is observed in all patients with the disease. The differential diagnosis should include those entities presenting\n\n<!-- p.814 -->\n\n#### **TABLE 36.3 Differential Diagnosis of Bartter Syndrome** \n\n|Leading Symptom|Differential Diagnosis|Additional Findings|\n|---|---|---|\n|Polyhydramnios of<br>|Aneuploidy|Abnormal karyotype|\n|fetal origin|Gastrointestinal tract malformation|Variable, empty stomach|\n||Congenital chloride diarrhea|Dilated intestinal loops|\n|Salt loss|Pseudohypoaldosteronism type 1|Metabolic acidosis, hyperkalemia|\n|Salt loss with<br>|Congenital chloride diarrhea|Low urinary chloride|\n|hypokalemic<br>alkalosis|Pseudo-Bartter syndrome (e.g., in CF)|Low urinary chloride|\n||Gitelman syndrome|Hypocalciuria, hypomagnesemia|\n||HNF1B nephropathy|Renal malformation, cysts, MODY5, hypomagnesemia|\n||HELIX syndrome|Hypercalcemia, hypohidrosis, ichthyosis|\n||Autosomal dominant hypocalcemia|Hypocalcemia, seizures|\n||EAST/SeSAME syndrome|Ataxia, seizures, deafness, developmental delay|\n||Surreptitious vomiting|Low urinary chloride|\n||Surreptitious laxative use|Low urinary chloride|\n||Surreptitious diuretic use|Highly variable urinary chloride|\n|Hypokalemic alkalosis<br>|Primary hyperaldosteronism;|Hypertension, low renin|\n|without salt loss|Apparent mineralocorticoid excess|Hypertension, low renin/aldosterone|\n||Liddle syndrome|Hypertension, low renin/aldosterone|\n|Nephrocalcinosis|Distal renal tubular acidosis|Metabolic acidosis|\n||Proximal tubular defects|No metabolic alkalosis|\n||Familial hypomagnesemia/hypercalciuria|No hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis, CKD|\n||Apparent mineralocorticoid excess|Hypertension, low renin/aldosterone|\n\n\n_CF,_ Cystic fibrosis; _CKD,_ chronic kidney disease; _EAST,_ epilepsy, ataxia, sensorineural deafness, tubulopathy; _HELIX,_ hypohidrosis, electrolyte imbalance, lacrimal gland dysfunction, ichthyosis, xerostomia; _HNF1B,_ hepatocyte nuclear factor 1 beta; _MODY5,_ maturity onset diabetes of the young type 5; _SeSAME,_ seizures, sensorineural deafness, ataxia, mental retardation, electrolyte imbalance. From Konrad M, Nijenhuis T, Ariceta G, et al. Diagnosis and management of Bartter syndrome: executive summary of the consensus and recommendations from the European rare kidney disease reference network working group for tubular disorders. _Kidney Int_ . 2021;99:324-335 (Table 4, p. 329). \n\nwith polyhydramnios (aneuploidy, gastrointestinal tract malformations, congenital chloride diarrhea) or salt loss (pseudohypoaldosteronism, Gitelman disease, autosomal dominant hypocalcemia) (Table 36.3). Pseudo-Bartter syndrome can be caused by increased Cl<sup>−</sup> elimination from the gastrointestinal tract (vomiting in pyloric stenosis, congenital chloride diarrhea, laxative abuse), losses through the skin (cystic fibrosis) or urine (diuretic treatment), and by deficits in Cl<sup>−</sup> intake (chloride-deficient formula feeding or almond milk). \n\n### **Prognosis** \n\nWith the use of indomethacin and additional supportive medication (potassium supplementation and spironolactone), the initial outcomes are good for classic Bartter syndrome. Even if patients’ potassium levels fail to normalize, with good adherence to treatment, they often remain \n\nrelatively asymptomatic and can achieve near-normal cognitive and musculoskeletal development. However, the longterm prognosis remains uncertain and the lack of rigorous therapeutic control, with the maintenance of chronic hypokalemia and hyperreninemia, may lead to the development of tubulointerstitial nephritis and slow progression to chronic renal failure. The main challenge, especially in the neonatal form, remains hypercalciuria, for which no effective therapeutic intervention is available. The prognosis in the neonatal form is thus more difficult to predict, given the possibility of developing nephrocalcinosis and progressive nephropathy. Some patients who have undergone renal transplantation have shown resolution of the initial symptoms. \n\n### **Treatment** \n\nThere is no curative treatment known for Bartter syndrome. Treatment strategies focus on the known and correctable\n\n<!-- p.815 -->\n\npathophysiology through the supplementation of renal electrolyte losses which play an important role in the disease pathophysiology itself (Fig. 36.2). \n\nThis means, on the one hand, replacing excess water and electrolytes eliminated by the kidney by increasing the oral intake, and on the other hand, inhibiting the activation of PGE2 to slow down salt loss. Adequate water intake and adequate loss replenishment must be ensured. \n\nTo assess salt loss, salt supplementation is recommended, in the form of sodium chloride (NaCl). This can support extracellular volume and improve electrolyte abnormalities. At least 5–10 mmol/kg/day has been recommended. A special situation in which sodium chloride supplementation is not recommended is in the case of patients with type 1 and type 2 Bartter diseases who have a secondary form of nephrogenic diabetes insipidus. In these patients, salt supplementation would worsen polyuria and risk hypernatremic dehydration. \n\nPotassium should also be supplemented, with supplementation in the form of potassium chloride being recommended, as other forms could worsen the metabolic alkalosis present in these patients. Potassium supplementation is almost mandatory due to the severity of the hypokalemia these patients may present and its associated life-threatening symptoms. The recommended dose being 1–3 mmol/kg/ day, the dose will be titrated according to an individual balance as the target level for plasma potassium is not exactly known. A reasonable target level may be 3 mmol/l, knowing that in some patients this might not be achievable and that we should not aim for normal potassium values. \n\nMagnesium supplementation should be added when hypomagnesemia is present (mainly in patients with type 3 Bartter disease), taking into consideration that organic salt (e.g., aspartate, citrate, lactate) have a higher bioavailability than magnesium oxide or hydroxide. Correction is typically difficult and target plasma magnesium levels in patients with Bartter disease are unknown but a level >0.6 mmol/L appears to be reasonable. \n\nThere is a need for consideration because urinary salt and electrolyte losses are continuous, ideal supplementation would be as close to continuous as possible. In addition, large variations in plasma levels may be more detrimental than subnormal but steady levels. Supplementation should be prescribed in as many doses as tolerable for the patient and in infants receiving continuous tube feeds, supplements should be added into feed and given continuously. \n\nMultiple clinical observational studies have shown benefit of suppressing prostaglandin formation in the form of improved growth and electrolyte profile. For pharmacological suppression of prostaglandin formation commonly used nonsteroidal antiinflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) are indomethacin (1–3 mg/kg/d divided in 3–4 doses) and ibuprofen (15–30 mg/kg daily in 3 doses) as nonselective inhibitors of COX enzymes, and celecoxib (2–10 mg/kg/d in 2 doses) as selective COX-2 inhibitor. There is insufficient evidence to recommend a specific NSAID. The use of selective COX-2 inhibitors has been reported in Bartter \n\nsyndrome, looking for a better side effect profile, but there is controversy about the evidence of usefulness in patients with Bartter disease. \n\nEuvolemia should be achieved before initiating NSAIDs, because volume status may affect the potential nephrotoxicity. It is recommended to start treatment after the fourth week of life or months of life in premature neonates, due to the increased risk of necrotizing enterocolitis and higher risk of decreasing glomerular filtration rate. Risks of cardiovascular and gastrointestinal side effects need to be considered individually and use of concomitant gastric acid suppressors is recommended. If proton pump inhibitors are used, there is a small risk of proton pump inhibitor associated hypomagnesemia that could compound renal magnesium wasting. Conversion to H2 blockers or other antacids (or to a COX-2 inhibitor) is recommended in those instances. \n\nTreatment results in improved height and weight, but catch-up growth is inadequate and there is persistent height retardation. Dietetic support is important to maximize caloric intake and facilitate optimal growth. Especially in infants and young children, tube feeding may need to be considered. Growth hormone deficiency has been demonstrated in some patients, with a positive effect of recombinant human hormone treatment in those cases. \n\nPotassium-sparing diuretics, angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, and angiotensin receptor blockers’ use in Bartter disease has been reported as treatment to ameliorate the electrolyte abnormalities. Regarding the use of potassium-sparing diuretics, it should be borne in mind that activation of the rennin-angiotensin-aldosterone axis occurs as a compensatory mechanism for salt loss as Bartter disease is primarily a salt-wasting disorder. Consequently, drugs that inhibit distal sodium reabsorption worsen the salt wasting and risk critical hypovolemia. These drugs should be considered carefully in individual cases and may be indicated in those who have severe symptoms from the electrolyte abnormalities despite maximization of routine treatment with NSAIDs and salt supplements. \n\nPerioperative management of patients with Bartter disease requires a particular care for volume repletion and correction of electrolyte abnormalities during anesthesia and the continuation of antiprostaglandin therapy to prevent the defective platelet aggregation. \n\nMaternal treatment for prenatal Bartter syndrome with NSAIDs has also been reported. However, this treatment has a risk to the fetus, especially of fetal ductus arteriosus constriction, and for neonatal intestinal perforation and necrotizing enterocolitis \n\n## **Gitelman Disease** \n\nGitelman disease (GD)—prevalence 1 in 40,000—has been considered a less severe mimic of Bartter syndrome due to its theoretical good prognosis and milder symptoms. Due to advances in genetics and molecular medicine, today GD is well differentiated and, increasingly, a better-known entity with differential clinical-analytical characteristics and\n\n<!-- p.816 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0817_02.jpg)\n\n\n\n![](figures/vfig_p0816_01.jpg)\n\n**Fig. 36.4** Clinical manifestations associated with Gitelman disease expressed by frequency of occurrence.  (Modified from Tsukamoto T, Kobayashi T, Kawamoto K, Fukase M, Chihara K. Possible discrimination of Gitelman’s syndrome from Bartter’s syndrome by renal clearance study: report of two cases. _Am J Kidney Dis._ 1995;25(4):637-641.) \n\nwide phenotypic variability. Moreover, it is the most frequently detected hereditary tubulopathy in adults. \n\nGitelman disease is caused by loss-of-function variants in _SLC12A3_ located on chromosome 16q13 and encoding the thiazide-sensitive Na<sup>+</sup> -Cl<sup>−</sup> co-transporter (NCC) of the distal tubule. \n\n### **Clinical Manifestations** \n\nOn the basis that GD is an entity that presents significant phenotypic variability, the main associated clinical manifestations (in order of frequency) are shown in Figure 36.4. \n\nA history of polyhydramnios is rare (oligohydramnios have occasionally been described) and infrequently (20%) produces symptoms in early childhood. Symptoms more commonly present during adolescence or adulthood as mild neuromuscular symptoms, typically paresthesias (especially in the face). In some cases, the diagnosis is incidental when serum tests performed for another reason identify the presence of hypokalemia. The presence and severity of symptoms has been related to the intensity of hypomagnesemia. \n\nThe most common symptoms are salt craving, muscle symptoms (fatigue, weakness, pain, cramps, paresthesia or numbness), palpitations, thirst (polydipsia), and the presence of low blood pressure. Another infrequent finding is the appearance **chondrocalcinosis** (Fig. 36.5), predominantly of the knees (the important role of magnesium in increasing the solubility of calcium pyrophosphate crystals \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0817_10.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 36.5** Image of chondrocalcinosis of the meniscus of the left knee.  (From García Nieto V, Cantabrana A, Müller D, Claverie-Martín F. [Condrocalcinosis e hipomagnesemia en un paciente portador de una nueva mutación en el gen del cotransportador de ClNa sensible a tiazidas]. _Nefrología_ . 2003;23(6):504-509.)\n\n<!-- p.817 -->\n\nleads to the possibility of deposition of these crystals in the joints, as well as in the sclera-sclerochoroidal calcifications). \n\nAlthough blood pressure reduction (variants in _SLC12A3_ have been shown to be protective against the development of hypertension) is constant at disease onset, with disease progression hypertension may develop in some cases, probably in relation to chronic activation of the renin angiotensinaldosterone system (RAAS) secondary to chronic salt loss. \n\nFailure to thrive and short stature are not common but have been described in some patients associated with a possible alteration of the growth hormone axis. The theoretical reduction of bone remodeling as a result of hypocalciuria and a positive calcium balance, could additionally contribute \n\nAnother less frequent manifestation but one that should be considered and actively sought in these patients because of its potential severity is the **lengthening of the corrected QT interval** , with the possibility of developing potentially fatal ventricular arrhythmias secondary to prolonged hypomagnesemia and hypokalemia. Isolated cases of seizures have been described in patients with GD (both in situations of hypomagnesemia and normomagnesemia); several studies have described the relationship with abnormalities in glucose metabolism and insulin secretion with the development of type 2 diabetes mellitus and chronic kidney disease in some patients. Episodes of **rhabdomyolysis** have also been described in cases of severe hypokalemia, with the possibility of secondary acute renal damage. \n\nDuring pregnancy, tubular losses of magnesium and potassium increase in patients with GD. It is not uncommon to find hypokalemia together with marked difficulty in normalization, despite replacement therapy. This correction, however, will not be necessary for a successful pregnancy. The infrequent and curious association between GD and glomerular pathology detected by the presence of proteinuria (even in the nephrotic range) reported in several cases deserves a separate point. A retrospective analysis performed in 36 patients with GD revealed the presence of proteinuria with preserved renal function in 16.6%. \n\n### **Pathology** \n\nThere is little histological data in patients with GD. Isolated cases reported no specific findings apart from occasional hypertrophy of the juxtaglomerular apparatus and marked reduction of NCC expression in immunohistochemical studies. \n\nIn the case of patients with GD in association with glomerular disease, there are only a few case reports of focal and segmental sclerosis. One notable case was associated with C1q nephropathy. \n\nHowever, the main differential findings in GD are hypomagnesemia (which could also be present in Bartter type 3) and hypocalciuria. The latter is the most consistent finding next to hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis and is defined as a calcium/creatinine ratio in isolated urine <0.2 mmol/mol (KDIGO guidelines). This determination is preferred to 24-hour urine because, although potentially more accurate, it presents the problem of incomplete collections, especially in pediatrics, and there is no evidence to specifically support its use. The presence of normomagnesemia is not uncommon. It is important to note that hypomagnesemia can develop progressively during childhood; its absence _does not_ exclude the disease. \n\nHypocalcemia has rarely been described. Attempts have been made to explain this finding by associating it with specific variants, such as homozygous frameshift variants of _SLC12A3_ . Hypocalcemia may be related to a decrease in the synthesis and release of parathyroid hormone as a consequence of a decrease in the activity of the calcium-sensitive receptor secondary to hypomagnesemia and/or to the positive calcium balance itself. \n\n### **Special Renal Function Tests** \n\nUnlike Bartter syndrome, salt and water loss are less marked in GD because urinary concentrating ability is preserved despite the direct effect of hypokalemia on urinary concentrating ability. \n\nThe application of hypotonic saline overload would have less effect than in the case of Bartter and is not performed in routine practice. In three patients studied during hypotonic saline diuresis, fractional distal chloride reabsorption was lower than that observed in normal subjects. In these studies, it was also shown that the increase in sodium, chloride, and magnesium excretion was equal to or higher than that of normal subjects in response to a single (adult) intravenous dose of furosemide (40 mg), and the increase in sodium excretion was lower than that of normal subjects in two patients treated with 500 mg of intravenous hydrochlorothiazide. The same was observed in another report in which patients showed a natriuretic response nullified after thiazide administration with a preserved response to furosemide. \n\nThe authors of this chapter independently demonstrated how hyposaline overload showed a dilution defect (minimum urinary osmolality 139 mOsm/kg) at the expense of an altered distal tubular reabsorption of Cl<sup>−</sup> (64.6%) and Na<sup>+</sup> (72.7%), as well as an elevated fractional excretion of K<sup>+</sup> (20.1%). This work demonstrated that the concentrating and acidification capacities in patients with GD were normal. \n\n### **Pathophysiology** \n\n### **Laboratory Findings** \n\nGD may share certain analytical alterations already described in Bartter syndrome, such as the presence of hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis or activation of the RAAS secondary to salt loss. \n\nThe complexities of the physiological mechanisms of transport in the distal convoluted tubule (Fig. 36.6) make it difficult, despite current knowledge in genetics and molecular biology, to fully understand all the symptoms observed in GD.\n\n<!-- p.818 -->\n\n![](figures/fig_p0819_02.jpg)\n\n\n**Fig. 36.6** Physiological mechanisms of transport in the distal convoluted tubule. Na<sup>+</sup> and Cl<sup>−</sup> pass from the tubular lumen into the cell via the thiazide-sensitive NaCl co-transporter NCC. Cl<sup>−</sup> leaves the cell via the chloride channel ClC-Kb (variants in the gene cause Bartter disease type 3). Na<sup>+</sup> leaves the cell via the Na<sup>+</sup> , K<sup>+</sup> -ATPase (variants in the gene encoding its ɣ-subunit produce autosomal dominant hypomagnesemia with hypocalciuria and those encoding the α-subunit hypomagnesemia with seizures and mental retardation type 2). Hepatocyte nuclear transcription factor 1 (HNF1β) regulates transcription of the gene encoding the ɣ-subunit of the Na<sup>+</sup> , K<sup>+</sup> -ATPase (variants in the coding gene produce HNF1β nephropathy). The coactivator PCBD1 (pterin-4-alpha-carbinolamine dehydratase 1) is a cofactor of HNF1β and increases the transcriptional activity of the γ-subunit of the Na<sup>+</sup> , K<sup>+</sup> -ATPase (variants in the coding gene produce transient neonatal Hyperphenylalaninemia with primapterinuria). FAM111A is a regulator of certain nuclear transcription factors (heterozygous missense variants in the coding gene cause Kenny-Caffey syndrome type 2, which can present with hypomagnesemia). In the distal tubule, Mg<sup>2+</sup> reabsorption is an active transport process by transcellular routes, driven by the potential difference between the tubular lumen and the interior of the cell. Mg<sup>2+</sup> ions pass from the tubular lumen to the cytosol via the apical TRPM6/TRPM7 channel (variants in _TRPM6_ and _TRPM7_ produce hypomagnesemia with autosomal recessive secondary hypocalcemia type 1 and type 2, respectively). They possibly exit the cell via CNNM2 (variants in the coding gene produce hypomagnesemia with seizures and mental retardation type 1) and the magnesium/sodium exchanger SLC41A1 (variants in the coding gene produce a nephronophthisis-like phenotype). EGF (epidermal growth factor) is an activator of the TRPM6 channel [variants in the coding gene produce autosomal recessive hypomagnesemia; variants in the EGF receptor (EGFR) produce neonatal inflammatory skin and gut disease type 2]. The apical force required for Mg<sup>2+</sup> transport is created by the cooperative action of the basolateral Na<sup>+</sup> , K<sup>+</sup> -ATPase, the chloride channel ClC-Kb, the K<sup>+</sup> -recycling heteromeric channel Kir4.1/Kir5.1 (variants in the gene encoding Kir 4. 1 produce EAST/SeSAME syndrome), the thiazide sensitive apical NaCl co-transporter NCC, and the K<sup>+</sup> extruder channels, ROMK (Kir1.1) (variants in the coding gene produce Bartter syndrome type 2) and Kv1.1 (variants in the coding gene produce autosomal dominant hypomagnesemia or episodic Ataxia type 1).  (Modified from García-Nieto VM, Claverie Martín F, Moraleda-Mesa T, et al. Renal diseases that course with hypomagnesemia. Comments on a new hereditary hypomagnesemic tubulopathy. _Nefrología_ . 2024;44(1):23-31 [Fig. 1, p. 26].)\n\n<!-- p.819 -->\n\nIn these patients, the observation that the electrolyte abnormalities resembled the effects produced by chronic administration of thiazides and the results obtained in clearance studies suggested that the defect must reside in the NCC co-transporter of the distal tubule. Although the exact reason for the usually mild clinical expression in comparison to Bartter syndrome is not yet known with complete certainty, both diseases being salt losing, the low fraction of salt reabsorption by the NCC in the distal convoluted tubule compared to that of the NKCC2 co-transporter of the loop of Henle (6% and 25%, respectively) could partly explain this difference. The involvement of this co-transporter could explain the salt loss, but not directly the resulting hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, or hypocalciuria. \n\nHypocalciuria is thought to occur because of a compensatory increase in proximal tubule passive calcium reabsorption (coupled to sodium) in response to volume depletion in the distal convoluted tubule; however, hypovolemia is unlikely to fully explain the increased calcium reabsorption, as volume expansion in patients with GD only partially reverses this phenomenon. Thus, it seems that another possible explanation is the outflow of calcium through the distal calcium transporter TRPV5 by the creation of an electrochemical gradient favored by the intracellular movement of sodium due to the absence of the apical NCC co-transporter. The implication of this mechanism is reinforced by the visualization of an increased abundance of these TRPV5 transporters in the disease in animal models. One study implicates voltage-dependent changes, calbindins, and other possible similarly functioning molecules that could communicate with calcium exit proteins on the basolateral side of the TCP cell through putative binding sites similar to their sensory and regulatory association with TRPV5. \n\nThe major and most consistent biochemical features are generated in the collecting tubule (CD). In a desperate attempt to recover as much sodium as possible that has not been reabsorbed in the distal tubule, the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) is upregulated at the apical membrane of the principal cells generating an influx of sodium into the cell through the electrochemical gradient established by the basolateral Na<sup>+</sup> -K<sup>+</sup> -ATPase (the TCD is the site in the nephron where it exhibits the greatest activity). Due to the need to establish an electrical balance, this process shares the simultaneous secretion of potassium through the ROMK channel in principal cells and the outflow of protons through the H<sup>+</sup> -ATPase in the type A-intercalated cell, producing the characteristic hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis. The possible hypochloremia observed results from the fact that sodium transport in the NCC is in the form of sodium chloride, whereas in the collector it is reabsorbed in isolation with a consequent increase in hydrogen chloride excretion. \n\nThese transport pathways is regulated by aldosterone and, together with volume depletion, are the reasons for the activation of the RAAS at the biochemical level. Given that, unlike Bartter syndrome, in GD the juxtaglomerular apparatus is not affected (preserving a normal glomerular filtration rate), the possible elevations of prostaglandins, renin, \n\nand aldosterone should reflect a physiological compensation of salt losses. \n\nThe causal mechanism for hypomagnesemia is less clear. It appears to be due to reduced activity of the epithelial magnesium channel TRPM6 of the apical membrane of the distal convoluted tubule through the already described increase in basolateral Na<sup>+</sup> -K<sup>+</sup> -ATPase activity and apical potassium conductance, which in turn facilitates magnesium uptake via TRPM6. \n\nWhy pregnant patients with GD have greater tubular losses of magnesium and potassium has been a matter of debate. The worsening of hypokalemia in this period could perhaps be explained by the increased activity of the RAAS during pregnancy. Likewise, possible mechanisms associated with cases accompanied by proteinuria could include angiotensin II- or renin-induced podocyte injury, as well as chronic hypokalemia. \n\n## **Etiology—Genetic Basis** \n\nGD is an autosomal recessive disease resulting from pathogenic variants in _SLC12A3_ (solute carrier family 12 member 3) located on chromosome 16q13 and composed of 26 exons, reflected in the absence of NCC transporter activity. The prevalence of heterozygous carriers is about 1% in the Caucasian population, being relatively higher in the Chinese population (2.9%). \n\nMore than 180 loss-of-function variants have been reported in this gene, the most frequent being missense variants and the least frequent being nonsense, frameshift, splice site defects or gene rearrangements. Most patients (75%) carry two _SLC12A3_ gene variants in compound heterozygosity, based on genomic DNA sequencing analysis of a large cohort. \n\nIn up to 40% of clinically diagnosed persons, only a single pathogenic variant is identified. The second variant could be located in regulatory fragments, deep intronic sequences, 5′ or 3′ untranslated regions, or be large genomic rearrangements. The presences of variants in other genes, such as _CLCNKB_ , have also been described, giving rise to overlap syndromes with simultaneous features of Bartter type 3 and GD. \n\nDeep intronic variants in _SLC12A3_ , together with the presence of large gene rearrangements (6%), make it advisable to include genetic testing such as multiplex ligation dependent probe amplification (MLPA) in these cases because it increases the sensitivity of diagnostic testing to >80%. \n\nThe most frequently detected pathogenic variants in _SLC12A3_ are: Thr60Met, Ala323Val, Asp486Asn, Gly741Arg, Leu859Pro, Arg861Cys, Arg928Gys, and Cys994Tyr. The variant Arg913Gln is associated with patients with GD and diabetic nephropathy in the context of type 2 diabetes mellitus. The very wide phenotypic heterogeneity of GD is not only limited to patients with different variants, but even occurs within patients of the same family and with the same primary genetic. The intrafamilial variability has been attributed to a\n\n<!-- p.820 -->\n\nmultifactorial interaction between the variant itself, tubular compensatory mechanisms, sex (often more severe in males), and environmental factors such as diet. \n\nThere are founder variants that appear in higher frequencies within certain populations, as is the case with the Arg642Cys or Thr180Lys seen in individuals of Japanese descent and c. 1180 + 1G>T seen in individuals within the Traveler community. This same group was identified to harbor a second founder variant, _SLC12A3_ , c.1939 G>A, in patients who do not carry the c.1180 + 1G>T. \n\n## **Diagnosis and Differential Diagnosis** \n\nThe diagnosis of GD is based on the clinical manifestations, the classic electrolyte alterations (hypokalemic and hypocalciuric metabolic alkalosis, usually together with hypomagnesemia), and genetic analysis, which should include MLPA and deep intronic sequencing in those patients in whom only a single variant is identified. It should also be remembered that, given the wide phenotypic variability in the disease, we can anticipate patients who are clinically asymptomatic (electrolyte abnormalities detected as an incidental analytical finding) to patients with severe symptoms. The most frequent presentation being mild muscular symptoms, especially in the form of paresthesias. \n\nDuring the diagnostic process (Fig. 36.7), we must take into account several syndromes with similar clinical-analytical presentations (especially with regard to the involvement of distal salt and magnesium transport) and which have in common, from the pathophysiological point of view, the alteration of Na<sup>+</sup> -K<sup>+</sup> -ATPase activity, either due to a decrease in sodium (GD) or potassium supply (EAST syndrome), altered regulation (FXYD2 or HNF1β), direct loss of function (ATP1A1), or deficient energy supply (mitochondrial diseases). Other entities such as Bartter syndrome type 3 can also present a very similar phenotype (25% with GD phenotype) and difficult to differentiate. \n\n**EAST syndrome** (previously called SeSAME) is a disease of autosomal recessive inheritance and whose name forms an acronym that includes each of the affectations that make up the disease: epilepsy, ataxia, sensorineural deafness, and Gitelman-like tubulopathy with or without the association of cognitive deficits. \n\nIt is caused by loss-of-function variants in _KCNJ10_ encoding the Kir4.1 channel, with a critical role in TCD (together with Kir5.1: gene _KCNJ16_ ), considered to be the renal “K<sup>+</sup> sensor.” Its function is to allow the recycling of K<sup>+</sup> in the cell to drive the activity of the Na<sup>+</sup> -K<sup>+</sup> -ATPase. The manifestations are indistinguishable from GD, although they appear late, but the severity is determined by \n\n\n![](figures/fig_p0821_09.jpg)\n\n\n\n![](figures/vfig_p0820_01.jpg)\n\n**Fig. 36.7** Diagnostic algorithm for hypomagnesemias based on calcemia and calciuria levels. Abnormal transporters are shown in parentheses (in most cases).  (Modified from García-Nieto VM, Claverie Martín F, Moraleda-Mesa T, et al. Renal diseases that course with hypomagnesemia. Comments on a new hereditary hypomagnesemic tubulopathy. _Nefrología_ . 2024;44(1):23-31 [Fig. 2, p. 28].)\n\n<!-- p.821 -->\n\nthe extrarenal manifestations: it presents as generalized seizures in infancy that improve with time (later focal epilepsy). Although _KCNJ10_ is expressed in the eye, there are no symptoms at this level. A tubulopathy with deafness associated with variants in _KCNJ16_ (Kir5.1) has been described. Kir5.1 forms heteromers with Kir4.2 _(KCNJ15)_ in the proximal tubule, playing an essential role in bicarbonate reabsorption and ammonia excretion. However, in this proximal and distal tubulopathy there are variants with a predominance of distal salt loss, generating a phenotype similar to GD in patients. \n\n**_MT-TI_** and **_MT-TF_** are involved in _mitochondrial_ pathology known for their involvement in other neurological syndromes. Specific variants, namely m.591C>T and m.4291T, have been related to Gitelman-like phenotypes (hypomag nesemia, hypokalemia, and hypocalciuria). These variants impact mitochondrial transfer RNA and demonstrate the essential role of mitochondria in sodium reabsorption, probably motivated by the high energy demand (the highest in the nephron) present in TCD. \n\nThe major phenotypic difference in the involvement of both genes is the development of progressive chronic kidney disease in patients affected by variants in the _MT-TF_ gene, whereas patients with _MT-TI_ variants maintain preserved renal function. Hypertension and dyslipidemia were associated in the initial descriptions, but their lack of consistency in the rest of the families described casts doubt on their relationship. \n\nThrough clinical studies it has been demonstrated how _HNF1b_ acts as a transcription factor in TCD controlling the expression of several proteins of the NCC regulatory pathway, including FXYD2 and KCNJ16. Alteration in the transcription of these reduces NCC activity. Minor radiological abnormalities present in the kidneys may lead to misdiagnosis. Hypomagnesemia and hypocalciuria may be the first symptom of the disease and are frequent (especially in heterozygous deletions and variants), whereas hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis is less frequent. Activation of the RAAS is scarce in these patients, unlike in GD, and chronic renal disease develops in 50%. Hypertension is present in 22% of patients. \n\n**_ATP1A1_** and **_FXYD2_** encode the α1 and γ subunits, respectively, of Na<sup>+</sup> -K<sup>+</sup> -ATPase. The α1 is a ubiquitous unit (also a neuronal maintenance function) and is responsible for generating the electrochemical gradient favorable for the reabsorption of salt and magnesium in the TCD. The γ subunit is responsible for increasing the affinity of the pump for ATP while decreasing the affinity for sodium, balancing energy utilization and maintaining the salt gradients. Both entities share a renal phenotype similar to GD, with severe renal loss of magnesium prevailing. \n\nHeterozygous _de novo_ variants in _ATP1A1_ result in severe hypomagnesemia with hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis and seizures with onset in infancy and resistance to treatment with magnesium and antiepileptic drugs. All develop global developmental delay and significant cognitive deficits. _FXYD2_ variants have been described in families with severe \n\nhypomagnesemia and childhood-onset seizures, coexisting with apparently healthy hypomagnesemic relatives. The hypocalciuria, tendency to hypokalemia, and clinical alkalosis (cramps, seizures, and chondrocalcinosis) is what resembles the picture to GD. \n\nMultiple cases have been reported in which an association of **Sjögren syndrome (SS)** with GD has been demonstrated, either as a concurrent manifestation of both disorders or as autoantibodies in SS against tubular co-transporters or that promote the clinical expression of GD in a heterozygous carrier patient (perform genetic analysis). What is important in this initial differentiation is the potential for response to the onset of immunosuppression in autoimmune variants. \n\n**HELIX syndrome** _(CLDN10)_ is caused by a deficiency of claudin-10b, a protein located in the tight junctions of the loop of Henle cells and involved in paracellular sodium absorption. Patients present hypochloremic and hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis with activation of the RAAS. Its major differential feature with GD is hypermagnesemia (compensatory action of claudins 16 and 19) and xerostomia. \n\n**RRAGD tubulopathy** _(RRAGD)_ is caused by activating variants of mTOR encoding a small Rag GTPase with probable action in the loop of Henle, due to the usual association with renal calcium loss. Although the main analytical alterations are very similar to GD (hypomagnesemia, hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis). A differentiating feature is that these patients present with nephrocalcinosis in association with cardiomyopathy. \n\nOther entities without RAAS activation or metabolic alkalosis but associated with chronic hypomagnesemia, such as those secondary to pathogenic variants in the _TRPM6, KCNA1, EGF, CNNM2_ or _PCBD1_ genes that can present with transient episodes of hypokalemia. \n\nIt is important to consider situations that may occur in routine clinical practice and that fall within the differential diagnosis of this entity, such as diuretic abuse (especially thiazides), patients with chronic vomiting or primary hyperaldosteronism (the latter with arterial hypertension and inhibition of the RAAS). \n\nOther drugs such as aminoglycosides (frequently used to treat premature neonates), proton pump inhibitors, laxatives, or cisplatin can also be accompanied by hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia, although metabolic alkalosis is usually absent. \n\n## **Prognosis** \n\nPatients with GD have an acceptable prognosis, although asthenia and muscle weakness are frequent daily manifestations that impact quality of life and call into question the mild nature that has classically been assigned to this disease. \n\nThe presence of QT interval changes or the long-term association of hypertension, type 2 diabetes mellitus, and chronic kidney disease should be considered and investigated to mitigate preventable impacts on health.\n\n<!-- p.822 -->\n\n## **Treatment** \n\nThere is no specific treatment for GD. Management is mainly based on ameliorating the effects of volume depletion and electrolyte correction through dietary guidelines and/or oral supplementation. A diet rich in sodium, potassium, and magnesium is clearly recommended regardless of the subsequent need for supplementation, although requirements will be highly variable. \n\nBecause magnesium has an inhibitory effect on potassium secretion mediated by the ROMK channel in the distal nephron, in case of magnesium deficiency more potassium is lost in the urine. This explains why, in patients with both disorders, we should first correct hypomagnesemia. \n\nSlow-release preparations of magnesium and potassium in chloride-based formulations are preferred (taken with meals), and it is recommended that potassium levels >3 mmol/L and magnesium >0.6 mmol/L be maintained as a target, although this should be individualized according to the needs of each patient (there is currently no clear consensus). Serum potassium levels <2 mmol/L could justify the need for intravenous replacement. Neither magnesium treatment in normomagnesemic patients nor oral sodium supplementation is currently recommended, although the latter is under study and might seem advisable due to the mechanism of the disease. Despite efforts to correct these electrolyte abnormalities, oral supplementation causes an increase in glomerular filtration and, therefore, an increase in renal losses, making it virtually impossible to normalize these levels in many patients. Intermittent intravenous administration has been proposed in these cases to alleviate symptoms; the effect is usually short-lived and their realizations are difficult. \n\nPotassium chloride and citrate, aspartate, lactate, or magnesium chloride are the most recommended formulas with the best bioavailability. The recommended starting (adult) dose is ≥12 mmol/day of magnesium and ≥40 mmol/day of potassium, in divided doses. Adjustments will be made based on symptoms, biochemical findings, and side effects. A key adverse effect of treatment is the occurrence of osmotic diarrhea in high-dose magnesium regimens. This situation will decrease absorption, and thus efforts should be made to adjust to the maximum dose of magnesium that does not produce diarrhea. Similarly, intercurrent processes that prevent adequate intake or absorption of the medication or its discontinuation could exacerbate the electrolyte abnormalities and trigger clinical symptoms. This is especially important in EAST syndrome because seizures have been linked to hypomagnesemia, although seizures have been reported in situations of normomagnesemia. \n\nMost pregnancies in GD occur without complications; however, fetal losses in the third trimester have been described. This could be due to exacerbation of electrolyte abnormalities, likely aided by the expansion of extracellular volume and increased renal clearance during gestation. It is therefore necessary to optimize treatment in this period. Use of amiloride has shown good control and favorable outcomes. \n\nThe only randomized controlled clinical trial conducted to date in GD compared the effectiveness of treatment with amiloride, indomethacin, and eplerenone, showing how moderate/severe hypokalemia was corrected in the short term, but only partially (normalization 20–25%), with 75 mg/day of slow-release indomethacin, 20 mg/day of amiloride, or 150 mg/day of eplerenone administered for 6 weeks (adult dosing) in combination with constant oral potassium and magnesium supplementation. Indomethacin was more effective than amiloride or eplerenone (with similar efficacy) and 73% who tolerated all three treatments responded to at least one of them. The effect on magnesium levels of all of these therapies was negligible. Glomerular filtration rate decreased significantly with indomethacin and less with amiloride (both reversible) but did not change with eplerenone. Tolerability was inversely proportional to its efficacy, with indomethacin being the least tolerated (gastrointestinal intolerance) leading to early discontinuation in 6/30 (20%), despite co-prescription of a proton pump inhibitor. This result suggests a role for PGE2 in the pathogenesis of GD. The beneficial effect may be due to increased NaCl reabsorption in the loop of Henle, which in turn leads to decreased NaCl delivery to the TCD and inhibition of renin release by the juxtaglomerular apparatus, as observed in rats treated with hydrochlorothiazide. \n\nThese treatments remain the last resort in patients with GD who continue to present with severe or symptomatic electrolyte abnormalities despite supplementation. Indomethacin has been recommended only in patients associated with joint pain due to chondrocalcinosis (which can be ameliorated with good magnesium management). \n\nACE inhibitors/ARBs can improve potassium control but have only been used as a last resort if all other treatments have failed, with limitation of dose increase due to the development of hypotension or renal damage. The beneficial effects of the use of growth hormone (GH) in patients with GD have been reported. \n\nIn one study, it was suggested that individualizing treatment in GD by monitoring renin concentration to reach the minimum dose at which it is possible to observe a beneficial effect. \n"});
